.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1122 AM PDT Fri Sep 4 2026
(Friday through Monday)

The primary concern over the Labor Day holiday weekend is hazardous
conditions at Pacific Coast beaches Friday through Monday. Two sets
of long period southerly swell from distant Hurricane Karina and
Hurricane Marie will result in an increased risk for sneaker waves
and strong rip currents with breaking waves up to 10 feet. Remaining
out of the water will not be enough, you will need to stay off of
beaches, jetties, and rocks to avoid being swept into the ocean by a
sneaker wave. Blue sky days are the most deadly at area beaches,
don`t further the narrative. Minor coastal flooding is expected
during high tide for bayshore locations along the San Pablo Bay
Friday afternoon/evening. Impacts can be expected primarily in
bayside Marin County. Sensible weather wise, anomalously deep upper-
level troughing will maintain below normal temperatures and onshore
flow through Sunday. The marine layer will likely be extremely
nuanced, especially today into tomorrow, as it has to form from
scratch after being mixed out by the front. Generally speaking, it
should be on the deeper side with the associated lift. In the
interim, ample low-level moisture and clear skies will make patchy
fog possible in the mornings. Gradual warming and drying trend kicks
off Monday as the low begins to fill and move east.

&&

.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1122 AM PDT Fri Sep 4 2026
(Tuesday through Thursday)

Upper-level ridging will nose in from the east on Tuesday, likely
bringing the warmest day of the week with temperatures rebounding to
near normal. A reinforcing trough is expected to dig well off the
West Coast and pickup what will likely be Tropical Depression Marie
Tuesday into Wednesday. This will turn the upper-level pattern into
a conveyor belt of moisture with cyclonic flow to the west and anti-
cyclonic flow to the east. As we are dealing with two moving
targets, the low and Marie, uncertainty is relatively high. It`s
important to note that Marie will not be making landfall in
California, let alone will it be tropical by the time it gets here.
We are simply going to get remnant moisture from it. The official
forecast has widespread rainfall up to 0.25" with locally higher
amounts up to 0.50" in the higher terrain. Right now, this looks
like a beneficial event with limited instability.

